Now I understand the current structure. Looking at the existing code, I can see that:

1. The project is using React with Vite
2. There's already a basic header with navigation links to different pages
3. There's a contact page component
4. Currently, navigation is done with simple anchor tags (`<a href="/...">`) which would cause full page reloads

For a proper routing system that allows SPA behavior (single-page application), I'll implement React Router. Let me first create an ADR for this decision:

TOOL REQUEST: write_file
{
  path: 'docs/adr/0009-use-react-router.md',
  content: '# ADR 0009: Use React Router for Client-Side Navigation\n' +
    '\n' +
    'DATE: 2024-05-21\n' +
    'STATUS: Accepted\n' +
    'TARGET MODULES: src/\n' +
    '\n' +
    '--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n' +
    '1. CONTEXT & PROBLEM STATEMENT\n' +
    '--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n' +
    'The current website uses simple anchor tags (`<a href="/...">`) for navigation between pages. This causes full page reloads which is not ideal for a modern web application experience. We need to implement client-side routing to enable SPA behavior, smooth transitions between pages, and proper URL handling without full page refreshes.\n' +
    '\n' +
    '--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n' +
    '2. DECISION\n' +
    '--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n' +
    'We will implement React Router v6 for client-side routing in this React application. This will allow us to:\n' +
    '- Create a single-page application with dynamic navigation\n' +
    '- Enable smooth transitions between pages\n' +
    '- Use proper URL paths that reflect the current page\n' +
    '- Maintain browser history and back/forward button functionality\n' +
    '\n' +
    '--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n' +
    '3. RATIONALE & ALTERNATIVES CONSIDERED\n' +
    '--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n' +
    'Why was this choice made over alternatives?\n' +
    '- Choice A: Continue with anchor tags (`<a href="...">`) - This approach causes full page reloads, which is not modern and provides a poor user experience.\n' +
    '- Choice B: Implement custom routing logic - While possible, it would be complex to implement properly and maintain. React Router is battle-tested and widely used.\n' +
    '- Choice C: Use a different routing library (like Reach Router) - React Router is the most popular and well-documented option for React applications.\n' +
    '\n' +
    'React Router v6 was chosen because:\n' +
    "- It's the current standard for React routing\n" +
    '- It has excellent documentation and community support\n' +
    '- It provides clean API with hooks like `useNavigate` and `useParams`\n' +
    '- It supports nested routes, lazy loading, and code splitting\n' +
    '\n' +
    '--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n' +
    '4. CONSEQUENCES\n' +
    '--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n' +
    'Positive:\n' +
    '- Smooth navigation between pages without full page reloads\n' +
    '- Proper URL handling that reflects current page state\n' +
    '- Better user experience with SPA behavior\n' +
    '- Support for browser history and back/forward buttons\n' +
    '- Easy to add new routes in the future\n' +
    '\n' +
    'Negative / Trade-offs:\n' +
    '- Additional dependency to manage\n' +
    '- Slight increase in bundle size\n' +
    '- Need to refactor existing navigation components to use React Router components\n' +
    '- Learning curve for developers unfamiliar with React Router concepts\n' +
    '================================================================================'
}

